--TEST--
`Core\IO::walk` names `fs.read` and is refused at the door for a directory the configuration does not cover — the refusal arrives instead of the value and not partway through a loop over it, so a program cannot count what it was not allowed to read — `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope` and `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`, spec § 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `fs.read` covers `open` and nothing else, so one member answers for one
// directory and refuses for its neighbour. `shut` is right there on disk with
// two entries in it: a member that asked only the filesystem would have walked
// it, and one that asked the door once the loop had started would have handed
// over an entry before refusing.
int $walked = 0;
foreach (Core\IO::walk("open") as string $entry) {
    $walked = $walked + 1;
}
echo "open: ", $walked as string, "\n";

int $seen = 0;
try {
    foreach (Core\IO::walk("shut") as string $entry) {
        $seen = $seen + 1;
    }
    echo "shut: walked\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    echo "shut: ", $denied->message, "\n";
}
echo "entries seen before the refusal: ", $seen as string, "\n";
--FILE open/one.txt--
one
--FILE open/nested/two.txt--
two
--FILE shut/three.txt--
three
--FILE shut/four.txt--
four
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["open"]
--EXPECTF--
open: 3
shut: Core\IO::walk needs the capability `fs.read` for %s%eshut, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`
entries seen before the refusal: 0
